## Lines 10–125 — The `<style>` block (CSS)

CSS lives inside `<style>` tags, still inside the `<head>`. It never shows on screen — it's purely instructions about appearance.

---

### CSS Variables (Lines 11–17)

```css
:root {
  --green-dark: #0d5c36;
  --green-mid:  #1a7a4a;
  --accent:     #f0a500;
  --danger:     #dc3545;
}
```

`:root` means "the whole page". These are **custom variables** — like giving names to colors so you don't repeat the same code everywhere.

Instead of writing `#0d5c36` 10 times, you write `var(--green-dark)` and if you ever want to change the color, you change it in **one place only**.

---

### A CSS Rule (Lines 44–50)

```css
.kpi-card {
  background: #fff;
  border-radius: 12px;
  padding: 18px 20px;
  box-shadow: 0 1px 4px rgba(0,0,0,.08);
  border-left: 4px solid var(--green-mid);
}
```

The pattern is always:

```
WHO  {  WHAT: VALUE;  }
```

- `.kpi-card` — the **who** (the dot means "any element with class kpi-card")
- Everything inside `{ }` — the **what** (background, corners, spacing, shadow, border)

| Property | What it does |
|---|---|
| `background: #fff` | White background |
| `border-radius: 12px` | Rounded corners |
| `padding: 18px 20px` | Space between content and the edge |
| `box-shadow: ...` | Soft shadow underneath |
| `border-left: 4px solid` | The green stripe on the left side of each card |

---

### Modifier classes (Lines 51–54)

```css
.kpi-card.profit { border-left-color: #198754; }
.kpi-card.cost   { border-left-color: #6c757d; }
.kpi-card.margin { border-left-color: var(--accent); }
```

These **override** just one property. A card with both classes `kpi-card profit` gets the green card style PLUS a different left border color. This is how you make variations without rewriting everything.

---

Any questions about CSS so far, or shall we move into the `<body>` — the visible part of the page?
